tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [INFO] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>A coworker constantly criticizes my work—advice?<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>That sounds really draining. When someone chips away at your efforts regularly, it can wear on your confidence and make even good days feel heavy.

A few thoughts that might help you navigate this:

**Take a quiet inventory first.** When you feel able, try to look at the criticism objectively. Is there any recurring thread that might be useful to you? Sometimes a difficult delivery contains a grain of something worth considering—and sometimes it’s just noise. Distinguishing the two can restore some of your emotional footing.

**Set gentle but firm boundaries if the delivery is harsh.** You might say something like, *“I want to do good work, and I’m open to feedback. I find I can take it in better when it’s specific and given one-to-one rather than in the moment—would that work for you?”* This invites collaboration without escalating tension.

**Document the pattern.** If the criticism feels excessive, personal, or undermining, keep a simple, factual record. It protects you if you ever need to discuss it with a manager or HR, and it can also help you see whether the behavior is truly pervasive or just feels that way because it stings.

**Consider a direct, calm conversation.** If you have enough rapport, you might name the dynamic without accusation: *“I’ve noticed a lot of critique coming my way, and I’m trying to understand what you’re hoping for.”* Sometimes people don’t realize how their tone lands; sometimes it opens a door; sometimes it reveals you need to involve others.

**Build your own validation network.** Check in with a trusted colleague or mentor about the quality of your work. External perspective from someone balanced can help you trust your own compass again.

You deserve to work in a space where feedback helps you grow rather than shrinks you. How long has this been going on, and is this person in any position of authority over you?<|im_end|>
